Duorhythm: EEG hyperscanning during dyadic joint rhythmic action
================================================================

Overview
--------
EEG hyperscanning dataset of pairs (dyads) of participants playing a cooperative
two-player rhythmic shooting game, in which partners coordinate the timing of
their shots, plus resting-state recordings. Ten dyads were recorded.

Each session simultaneously recorded both members of a dyad on a single Brain
Products amplifier as one 129-channel stream: participant 1 on a 64-channel cap
(Fp1..Iz), participant 2 on a second 64-channel cap (Fp1_2..Iz_2 in the source),
and one (unused) marker-index channel. In this BIDS release each dyad is split
into TWO subjects so that per-participant metadata maps cleanly and standard
single-subject tooling works. Because both caps share one amplifier clock, the
two members of a pair are sample-aligned: sample i of one subject and sample i of
its partner are the same instant. Pair membership is in participants.tsv
(`pair`, `partner`).

Subjects and pairing
--------------------
- 10 dyads -> 20 subjects (sub-01 .. sub-20).
- pair-0k contains sub-(2k-1) [participant 1] and sub-(2k) [participant 2].
- See participants.tsv for the `pair`/`partner` links and self-reported
  gender / age band / acquaintance_length (how long the pair had known each other).

Tasks / runs (per subject)
--------------------------
- task-duorhythm                  : the cooperative shooting game (~21 min, 1000 Hz).
- task-rest acq-pre, acq-post     : resting-state recordings before (pre) and after
                                    (post) the task (no behaviour recorded).

The two rests use the acq-pre / acq-post label rather than run-01/02 because they
are not interchangeable repetitions: one is recorded before the joint task and one
after. Keeping a single task-rest (so tools find all resting-state) while marking
the timepoint with acq- makes the pre/post structure explicit and queryable.

Task structure and conditions
-----------------------------
The game is organised into 10 blocks x 9 trials = 90 trials of 12 s each. Each
block contains 3 trials of each of three target types (so 30 trials per condition
overall). On each trial both participants shoot a target together; the target
type sets how their shots must be timed:

  trial_type    code  target  behaviour
  alternating    1    black   partners shoot alternately (AA / alternating action)
  simultaneous   2    green   partners shoot simultaneously (SA / simultaneous action)
  free           3    blue    partners shoot freely, no timing constraint (F)

(An in-game white "Bait" circle penalises the score and is not a trial type.)

Events
------
sub-*_task-duorhythm_events.tsv contains, per participant:
- 90 trial rows: onset, duration (~12 s), trial_type (above), value (raw trigger
  code), trial_number.
- button-press rows (trial_type = "buttonpress"): every shot fired by THAT
  participant, with `shot_target` = the target struck (0 = miss; 1-3 = the condition's
  colour, matching the trial_type table above -- 1 black, 2 green, 3 blue; 4 = rare
  white "Bait" distractor) and the `trial_number` it falls in. Values 1-3 equal the active
  condition code (derived empirically from shot-vs-condition co-occurrence, all 24,012 shots).
Column definitions are in task-duorhythm_events.json. Trial timing comes from the
behavioural "Task" stream trigger channel and is mapped to the nearest EEG sample
(the same alignment the lab used to epoch rawdata_trial_mat); reconstructed trial
labels reproduce rawdata_trial_mat's `trialtype` exactly for all 10 sessions.

Continuous behaviour (beh/)
---------------------------
The continuous game signals (100 Hz) are stored per participant in the behavioural
(beh/) datatype as a BIDS continuous recording:
  sub-XX/beh/sub-XX_task-duorhythm_recording-gamedata_physio.tsv.gz  (+ .json)
Columns: crosshair_x / crosshair_y, crosshair_vx / crosshair_vy, shot_confirm,
shot_target, and the shared block score. Crosshair positions (crosshair_x/_y) are the
game's 2D Unity world coordinates (origin-centred floats); velocities (crosshair_vx/_vy)
are their per-second time-derivatives (Unity world units per second). SamplingFrequency
= 100 Hz; StartTime aligns the sample grid to the EEG via the shared LSL clock. The beh/ folder marks this as
behaviour; the file uses BIDS' continuous-recording suffix `_physio` (there is no
continuous time-series `_beh` suffix), with `recording-gamedata` + the JSON Columns
carrying the meaning. (Discrete trial and shot events are in eeg/…_events.tsv; this is
the continuous form, mirroring the EEG per-participant split.)

EEG acquisition
---------------
- Amplifier: Brain Products BrainAmp; cap: EASYCAP "Standard 64Ch actiCAP snap"
  (order code CACS-64), 64 channels in the standard extended 10/10 layout
  (EEGPlacementScheme).
- No electrode-position files (electrodes.tsv / coordsystem.json) are included, because
  electrode positions were not digitised for individual participants. BrainVision files
  store channel labels only, not 3D coordinates; the layout is the standard cap template,
  fully specified by the channel names. To obtain template coordinates, apply MNE's
  `easycap-M1` montage. This has been verified against the manufacturer's cap document
  (CACS-64): all 64 channel names and their order match the published layout exactly, and
  they also match MNE's `easycap-M1` template and the lab's chanlocs.mat one-for-one.
- Sampling frequency: 1000 Hz. Data stored in microvolts (BrainVision float).
- Online reference: FCz. Ground: Fpz. Impedance kept below 25 kOhm.
- Hardware band-pass: 0.016 - 1000 Hz. Mains 60 Hz (a 60 Hz notch is applied in
  the lab's preprocessing, not in this raw release; SoftwareFilters = n/a).
- Recording dates were dropped during conversion, so the data carry no acquisition
  timestamps.

Questionnaire (phenotype/)
--------------------------
The self-report questionnaire has five parts. Part 1 (demographics + relationship) is in participants.tsv. Parts
2-5 are four distinct 9-item Likert subscales (1 = Strongly Disagree .. 7 =
Strongly Agree), each its own phenotype file (one row per participant; items are
positional item_01..item_09 with the exact wording in the matching .json):
- phenotype/display_comfort.tsv      (Part 2, pre-experiment)
- phenotype/game_familiarity.tsv     (Part 3, pre-experiment)
- phenotype/coordination_skill.tsv   (Part 4, post-experiment)
- phenotype/coordination_level.tsv   (Part 5, post-experiment)

Data-quality notes (survey / questionnaire)
-------------------------------------------
Resolved with Eric Loh (2026-06-24); concerns the Likert subscales only --
demographics in participants.tsv are retained:
- sub-06 (0116_2): the survey was restarted, so the real response begins partway
  down the file (at the demographics row). The pre-restart rows are discarded and
  the four subscales use the post-restart blocks. Complete.
- sub-02, sub-04, sub-10, sub-20 (all P2): only the pre-experiment half of the
  questionnaire was recorded (software logging limitation + procedural oversight).
  The pre-experiment subscales (display_comfort = Part 2, game_familiarity = Part 3)
  are retained; the post-experiment subscales (coordination_skill = Part 4,
  coordination_level = Part 5) are n/a (see KnownIssues in those phenotype JSONs).
- sub-16 (0318_2): logged in a different file format (unquoted, comma-padded); the
  protocol flagged it as possibly mis-logged. Its four blocks parse normally and are
  included; not specifically addressed in Eric's review.

Provenance / conversion
-----------------------
Converted from the original Lab Streaming Layer XDF recordings with
the pipeline notebooks (`1_eeg_to_bids.ipynb` for EEG, `2_build_events.ipynb` for events, `3_build_physio.ipynb` for continuous game data, `4_build_phenotype.ipynb` for the questionnaire), using pyxdf + MNE +
MNE-BIDS, following the lab's documented method (Confluence "Data collection and
storage"; Chen Lam "Eric" Loh's PhD thesis appendix). EEG conversion splits the
128 EEG channels into the two participants (rows 1-64 = P1, 65-128 = P2; the
flat `MkIdx` channel is dropped), converts microvolts to volts, and applies the
easycap-M1 montage. Original raw data live at /bucket/FroeseU/duorhythm. Source
artefacts not in this release (epoched data in rawdata_trial_mat/, the full
behavioural Task stream, survey CSVs) remain with the lab.

Known limitations
-----------------
- No electrode-position files (electrodes.tsv / coordsystem.json) are included:
  electrode positions were not digitised. The montage is the standard EasyCap M1
  template (extended 10/10), fully given by the channel names; apply MNE's
  `easycap-M1` to obtain template coordinates.

Ethics: approved by the OIST Human Subjects Research Review Committee, approval
no. HSR-2024-019-5.

